WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.080
Isn't that a movie
about insider trading?

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.560
Yep. But it's the...

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.800
Well, the whole point is

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.600
that it helps us think
about all the dangers that,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:14.820
that we need to protect the markets.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.760
Well, you want to tackle
the issues head on, right?

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.940
Right. Exactly.
And you can also take a joke, I guess.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:20.950
Yeah,
Exactly. That

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.950
too

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.020
I guess. Tarek Mansour

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.920
and Luana Lopes Lara are
the co-founders of Kalshi,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.220
a prediction market platform

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.540
where you can bet on
everything from today's weather

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.620
to Taylor Swift's wedding.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.960
But back in 2018, when
they founded their company,

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.140
very few people outside

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.820
of academia had heard
of prediction markets.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.340
These days, Kalshi seems to be everywhere

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.000
with an in-your-face presence
that's hard to ignore.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.480
Buzz, controversy, and a ton of money.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.620
Billions of dollars in trades.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.560
These are fundamentally corrupt markets.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.900
75% of people lose money.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.520
Do you feel a moral obligation

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.700
to be more transparent about that?

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.100
I think we are very transparent

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.680
that, you know, there are risks.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.460
People lose money and people win money.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.040
Its blockbuster growth has
been fueled by a generation

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.100
of users drawn in by
the lure of easy money.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.360
Why isn't Kalshi a casino in your pocket?

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.460
That's like same as
asking like why is AI

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.540
good for society, right?

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.840
Like these are large complex topics

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.320
that cannot be summarized in a soundbite.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.560
But as Kalshi continues
on its trajectory,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.960
it's now facing a host of new
competitors, local opponents,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.900
and a very public debate
about what this platform is

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.180
and the potential harms it can cause.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.200
It can feel a little bit like being in

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.220
the middle of a hurricane sometimes.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.640
Yeah. What speed are
the winds blowing today?

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.960
When I get to the office, we'll see.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.840
Category five?
Yeah, maybe. Maybe.

00:01:52.481 --> 00:01:54.781
So this is your old walk to Goldman?

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.261
Yeah. I really loved it.

00:01:56.261 --> 00:01:58.461
I mean, it was really cool
and it's all buildings

00:01:58.461 --> 00:01:59.641
and finance

00:01:59.641 --> 00:02:02.041
and I'm a math geek, so you
know, it's like really kind

00:02:02.041 --> 00:02:04.101
of a very exciting time.

00:02:04.101 --> 00:02:05.961
How does a mathematical mind see the

00:02:05.961 --> 00:02:07.901
world that the rest of us don't?

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:11.061
So growing up in Lebanon, it
was a very volatile country.

00:02:11.061 --> 00:02:12.461
The delta, like the, the difference

00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:13.821
between good and bad can be an hour.

00:02:13.821 --> 00:02:16.281
All this chaos outside and this volatility

00:02:16.281 --> 00:02:18.021
and it was just like hard to stomach.

00:02:18.021 --> 00:02:20.081
What it made me do is view the world

00:02:20.081 --> 00:02:21.841
essentially more probabilistically.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.841
That has sort of in some ways

00:02:23.841 --> 00:02:25.121
propelled every decision I made

00:02:25.121 --> 00:02:26.141
all the way to starting Kalshi.

00:02:27.101 --> 00:02:29.641
Mansour left Lebanon to attend MIT

00:02:29.641 --> 00:02:31.761
and then became a trader on Wall Street.

00:02:31.761 --> 00:02:33.881
In 2016, he watched as big banks

00:02:33.881 --> 00:02:36.501
and hedge funds scramble to offset risk.

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:38.701
It was all about like
Brexit and the Trump election

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:40.461
and everybody was super
stressed about that

00:02:41.421 --> 00:02:42.661
and you could feel the anxiety.

00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:44.141
No one saw it coming though.

00:02:44.141 --> 00:02:45.641
Yeah, that's the whole thing, right?

00:02:45.641 --> 00:02:47.101
A bunch of the sort of institutional

00:02:47.101 --> 00:02:48.861
investors were saying
like, oh, what do we do?

00:02:48.861 --> 00:02:52.021
How do we kind of get a
position on Trump winning?

00:02:52.021 --> 00:02:53.701
What's a Trump trade?
But this is the thing

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:55.081
that really got me excited about, well,

00:02:56.481 --> 00:02:58.741
what if these investors could just buy

00:02:58.741 --> 00:02:59.861
yes on Trump winning the election?

00:03:00.861 --> 00:03:01.921
That just seems like a much more

00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:04.021
direct, simple way to do it.

00:03:05.181 --> 00:03:07.321
Kalshi is a peer-to-peer platform.

00:03:07.321 --> 00:03:09.641
Every trade is a contract, a simple yes

00:03:09.641 --> 00:03:12.061
or no on whether something will happen.

00:03:12.061 --> 00:03:14.721
By the yes, and somewhere
on the other side,

00:03:14.721 --> 00:03:15.961
someone is betting you're wrong.

00:03:17.121 --> 00:03:20.121
The price of that contract
moves in real time based on

00:03:20.121 --> 00:03:21.261
what the market believes.

00:03:22.441 --> 00:03:24.841
Kalshi makes money by
charging a transaction

00:03:24.841 --> 00:03:26.021
fee on every trade.

00:03:27.201 --> 00:03:29.021
So Kalshi means everything in Arabic.

00:03:29.021 --> 00:03:31.261
Yeah.
What does that say about your ambitions?

00:03:31.261 --> 00:03:32.961
We want to build a
financial market that is sort

00:03:32.961 --> 00:03:35.001
of anyone can basically participate in it.

00:03:35.001 --> 00:03:37.281
That doesn't mean
everybody's going to win,

00:03:37.281 --> 00:03:38.942
but you have a shot to,
if you do the research,

00:03:38.942 --> 00:03:40.362
if you learn, if you dig into topics,

00:03:40.362 --> 00:03:42.802
you get into rabbit
holes, you can get better.

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:45.742
So keep trying? I mean, should
anyone stop? I don't know.

00:03:45.742 --> 00:03:48.302
I mean, I think 75% of
our customers don't trade.

00:03:48.302 --> 00:03:50.482
They use it for the forecast.

00:03:50.482 --> 00:03:51.882
And the thing that they like about it is

00:03:51.882 --> 00:03:55.002
because in some ways prediction
markets are sort of one

00:03:55.002 --> 00:03:57.642
of the only non-elite
sources of information.

00:03:57.642 --> 00:03:59.542
When you look at prediction
markets, it's bottom up.

00:03:59.542 --> 00:04:02.162
It's people that are essentially
putting skin in the game,

00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:03.462
money where their mouth is, and

00:04:03.462 --> 00:04:05.242
so people find the data
very, very valuable.

00:04:10.822 --> 00:04:11.922
Have you been here before?

00:04:11.922 --> 00:04:13.682
I haven't.
Oh, it's the first time?

00:04:13.682 --> 00:04:15.242
Yeah. What's the vibe?

00:04:15.242 --> 00:04:16.762
How would you describe the culture here?

00:04:16.762 --> 00:04:17.922
Right now it's pretty calm, I think.

00:04:17.922 --> 00:04:19.902
Culture is everybody's working
and they're very focused.

00:04:20.842 --> 00:04:23.182
So good to see you.
It's so good to see you.

00:04:23.182 --> 00:04:24.182
Thanks so much for having us.

00:04:24.182 --> 00:04:26.782
Of course, of course.
Thank you for having us.

00:04:26.782 --> 00:04:28.022
Mansour's co-founder

00:04:28.022 --> 00:04:32.002
and Kalshi COO, Luana Lopes Lara
had an unorthodox path

00:04:32.002 --> 00:04:33.062
to the world of fintech.

00:04:34.222 --> 00:04:36.662
Growing up in Brazil, she
trained as a ballerina

00:04:36.662 --> 00:04:39.322
before pivoting to math
and computer science.

00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:40.282
There are a lot of stories out there.

00:04:40.282 --> 00:04:41.115
What's true or not true?

00:04:42.842 --> 00:04:43.922
Well, there's a lot of untrue.

00:04:45.382 --> 00:04:46.522
There's a lot of untrue. Yeah,

00:04:46.522 --> 00:04:47.782
it's like you can start there.

00:04:47.782 --> 00:04:49.742
So you met at MIT.
Yep.

00:04:50.762 --> 00:04:53.482
There's a story that
Luana is the smart one

00:04:53.482 --> 00:04:56.502
and that you used to
try to sit next to her

00:04:56.502 --> 00:04:57.942
to learn her study habits.

00:04:57.942 --> 00:04:59.922
She was more nerdy.
Tarek was the type of guy

00:04:59.922 --> 00:05:01.662
that tried to seem very cool.

00:05:01.662 --> 00:05:03.562
I knew I wasn't cool. I'm like,
"I'm not going to be cool.

00:05:03.562 --> 00:05:05.462
I might as well just study

00:05:05.462 --> 00:05:07.382
and be nerdy and sit in the front row."

00:05:07.382 --> 00:05:10.022
I also heard you used to date. True?

00:05:10.022 --> 00:05:11.882
Long time ago. Yes, that's true.

00:05:11.882 --> 00:05:13.662
How did you get beyond
that to launch a starter?

00:05:13.662 --> 00:05:15.202
Well, the company. The
company always comes first

00:05:15.202 --> 00:05:16.622
for us, essentially.

00:05:16.622 --> 00:05:18.622
And by the time we started the company,

00:05:18.622 --> 00:05:19.862
it was basically already fizzled out.

00:05:20.722 --> 00:05:23.462
Company above all else, and you
know it's worked out for us.

00:05:24.462 --> 00:05:26.403
In 2020, in the final months

00:05:26.403 --> 00:05:28.083
of the first Trump administration,

00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:30.863
they cleared their first major
hurdle when they got approval

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:32.963
from the Commodity
Futures Trading Commission

00:05:32.963 --> 00:05:35.223
to become the first federally regulated

00:05:35.223 --> 00:05:37.163
event contract exchange.

00:05:37.163 --> 00:05:38.643
But Kalshi was limited to the kinds

00:05:38.643 --> 00:05:41.123
of markets the agency had
traditionally regulated.

00:05:42.083 --> 00:05:44.023
When they tried to expand into elections,

00:05:44.023 --> 00:05:47.963
the CFTC then under the
Biden administration said no,

00:05:47.963 --> 00:05:49.863
calling election betting a form of gaming.

00:05:51.323 --> 00:05:53.863
So Kalshi decided to
sue its own regulator.

00:05:55.143 --> 00:05:58.263
What made you so sure
that was the right move?

00:05:58.263 --> 00:06:01.243
It was kind of the only
option we had in a lot of ways,

00:06:01.243 --> 00:06:03.783
because if we don't do it, these
markets might never see the

00:06:03.783 --> 00:06:05.943
light of day because we had
another company that tried

00:06:05.943 --> 00:06:07.103
to do it and do it.

00:06:07.103 --> 00:06:08.423
So it was a sense of responsibility there,

00:06:08.423 --> 00:06:09.743
but also because we were right.

00:06:10.623 --> 00:06:12.103
In 2024, just weeks

00:06:12.103 --> 00:06:13.903
before the presidential election,

00:06:13.903 --> 00:06:16.643
Kalshi won its lawsuit against the CFTC

00:06:16.643 --> 00:06:19.383
and started offering markets on elections.

00:06:19.383 --> 00:06:21.363
Prediction markets were
thrust into the national

00:06:21.363 --> 00:06:23.983
conversation as betters rushed in.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:26.603
And while polls showed Kamala
Harris and Donald Trump neck

00:06:26.603 --> 00:06:31.543
and neck, Kalshi's markets
gave Trump over 60% odds.

00:06:31.543 --> 00:06:33.063
In the weeks around the election,

00:06:33.063 --> 00:06:36.943
Kalshi processed over a hundred
million dollars in trades.

00:06:36.943 --> 00:06:38.883
Looking at our billboard
right in Times Square.

00:06:40.023 --> 00:06:43.323
Wow.
We realized with obviously the number

00:06:43.323 --> 00:06:45.783
of users coming in, the
volume, how much it was cited

00:06:45.783 --> 00:06:50.083
by the media, we realized,
oh wow, it's like actually,

00:06:50.083 --> 00:06:52.243
it actually might be
happening for real now.

00:06:52.243 --> 00:06:54.103
With Donald Trump
back in the White House,

00:06:54.103 --> 00:06:56.483
prediction markets that the
Biden administration flagged

00:06:56.483 --> 00:06:58.843
for review were suddenly fine.

00:06:58.843 --> 00:07:01.423
Since then, the bets have kept on coming.

00:07:02.363 --> 00:07:04.563
The breakneck growth of
prediction markets has been led

00:07:04.563 --> 00:07:06.883
by two players, Kalshi

00:07:06.883 --> 00:07:10.743
and its arch rival, Polymarket
led by CEO Shane Copeland.

00:07:12.023 --> 00:07:13.763
And the hostility between Mansour

00:07:13.763 --> 00:07:15.584
and Copeland is well documented.

00:07:16.644 --> 00:07:18.664
How bad is the bad blood?

00:07:18.664 --> 00:07:20.724
It really isn't that bad.
It certainly seems bad if you

00:07:20.724 --> 00:07:22.704
read the tabloids or even tweets.

00:07:22.704 --> 00:07:24.864
The tabloids are, you know,
the tabloids love drama.

00:07:24.864 --> 00:07:26.084
I mean, they're tabloids, right?

00:07:26.084 --> 00:07:27.324
Like it all is centered

00:07:27.324 --> 00:07:29.864
around, you know, doing the right thing.

00:07:29.864 --> 00:07:33.664
And when you have an actor that
essentially doesn't do that,

00:07:33.664 --> 00:07:36.924
that can basically like sully
the industry a little bit.

00:07:36.924 --> 00:07:39.384
The coverage is it doesn't
say Polymarket did X, Y, Z.

00:07:39.384 --> 00:07:41.364
It says prediction
markets did X, Y, and Z.

00:07:41.364 --> 00:07:42.704
And we are put in a position
where you always have

00:07:42.704 --> 00:07:43.684
to explain to people that like, no,

00:07:43.684 --> 00:07:44.604
we're not doing war markets.

00:07:44.604 --> 00:07:46.184
We're not doing death markets.

00:07:46.184 --> 00:07:49.424
Polymarket didn't respond
to our request for comment.

00:07:49.424 --> 00:07:51.624
The company says it
prohibits insider trading

00:07:51.624 --> 00:07:52.864
and manipulation and

00:07:52.864 --> 00:07:55.564
that Kalshi's ads are
misinforming the public,

00:07:55.564 --> 00:07:59.164
which only harms the industry
as a whole and participants.

00:07:59.164 --> 00:08:01.404
Kalshi says it doesn't
offer markets on death,

00:08:01.404 --> 00:08:05.244
assassinations or war in
keeping with US regulations,

00:08:05.244 --> 00:08:07.504
but playing by the rules
doesn't mean Kalshi is

00:08:07.504 --> 00:08:09.164
content to play it safe.

00:08:09.164 --> 00:08:11.304
Their ads make that pretty clear.

00:08:11.304 --> 00:08:13.484
If history has taught
us anything, it's not

00:08:13.484 --> 00:08:14.844
to root against the underdog.

00:08:17.824 --> 00:08:19.924
Yet as the number of
prediction markets has grown,

00:08:19.924 --> 00:08:21.484
so has the temptation for some

00:08:21.484 --> 00:08:23.824
to trade on insider information.

00:08:23.824 --> 00:08:26.564
Federal regulators said a White
House teleprompter operator

00:08:26.564 --> 00:08:29.904
made over $100,000 by placing
bets on Trump's speeches.

00:08:30.824 --> 00:08:33.904
Former US Representative
George Santos posted on social

00:08:33.904 --> 00:08:37.164
media that he would show up
for the state of the Union.

00:08:37.164 --> 00:08:39.624
Meanwhile, he placed
multiple bets on Kalshi

00:08:39.624 --> 00:08:43.404
that he would not attend,
making over $17,000.

00:08:43.404 --> 00:08:44.584
I'm going to be in the gallery.

00:08:44.584 --> 00:08:47.244
I never said I was going to
be on the floor or maybe.

00:08:48.304 --> 00:08:50.404
The CFTC called it manipulation

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:51.884
and Kalshi banned him for life.

00:08:53.084 --> 00:08:54.504
I know you are very busy

00:08:54.504 --> 00:08:58.524
because there is not a
lull in these markets.

00:08:58.524 --> 00:09:00.105
Bloomberg reporters Christopher Beam

00:09:00.105 --> 00:09:03.525
and Denitsa Tsekova have been
covering the explosive rise

00:09:03.525 --> 00:09:06.465
of prediction markets day in and day out.

00:09:06.465 --> 00:09:09.505
How many more cases like
this are we likely to see?

00:09:09.505 --> 00:09:10.805
I think it's inevitable.

00:09:10.805 --> 00:09:12.445
The incentives are there

00:09:12.445 --> 00:09:13.685
and when the incentives are there,

00:09:13.685 --> 00:09:15.485
someone's going to take advantage of it.

00:09:15.485 --> 00:09:20.125
Kalshi says that they are
cracking down on insider trading,

00:09:20.125 --> 00:09:21.665
cracking down on manipulation,

00:09:21.665 --> 00:09:25.905
but you also have to wonder
how far that's going to extend.

00:09:25.905 --> 00:09:26.965
As those markets grow

00:09:26.965 --> 00:09:29.765
and as the group of people who
have that information grows,

00:09:29.765 --> 00:09:33.365
I can see how this is harder
and harder to prosecute.

00:09:33.365 --> 00:09:35.785
Quick game. Great.
Insider versus expert.

00:09:35.785 --> 00:09:37.925
A White House staffer on a
presidential announcement.

00:09:38.905 --> 00:09:40.885
That would be an insider.
A player's girlfriend

00:09:40.885 --> 00:09:42.325
or boyfriend on injury status.

00:09:43.285 --> 00:09:44.125
Well, one, we'd never do this

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:45.525
market and two, that would be an insider.

00:09:45.525 --> 00:09:47.425
A Federal Reserve
employee on interest rates.

00:09:49.465 --> 00:09:52.205
If they have knowledge
beforehand of what they're going

00:09:52.205 --> 00:09:54.445
to decide, definitely insider.

00:09:54.445 --> 00:09:56.465
So if you have a market
for just about everything,

00:09:56.465 --> 00:09:58.025
almost everyone can be an insider.

00:09:58.025 --> 00:10:00.005
Like can't anything be gamed?

00:10:00.005 --> 00:10:02.305
Manipulation and insider
trading exists in all financial

00:10:02.305 --> 00:10:04.245
markets, the same way that fraud

00:10:04.245 --> 00:10:06.325
and cheating exists in any system.

00:10:06.325 --> 00:10:08.445
The key thing is how do
you regulate that system

00:10:08.445 --> 00:10:11.205
to make sure you root out
the insiders and the cheaters

00:10:11.205 --> 00:10:12.585
and establish fair rules of the road so

00:10:12.585 --> 00:10:13.805
that people can participate fairly?

00:10:14.685 --> 00:10:17.265
Yet the CFTC, the very
agency that's supposed

00:10:17.265 --> 00:10:19.785
to police all of this,
has been hollowed out.

00:10:19.785 --> 00:10:21.885
Under President Trump, the
five-person commission is

00:10:21.885 --> 00:10:23.345
now down to one man.

00:10:23.345 --> 00:10:24.885
Chair Michael Selig,

00:10:24.885 --> 00:10:26.865
a Trump appointee who's acted more like a

00:10:26.865 --> 00:10:28.805
champion than a watchdog.

00:10:28.805 --> 00:10:30.945
This is financial market
activity, so it's something

00:10:30.945 --> 00:10:33.185
that I think is valuable to society.

00:10:33.185 --> 00:10:34.565
Meanwhile, the markets he's supposed

00:10:34.565 --> 00:10:36.545
to be watching keep expanding.

00:10:36.545 --> 00:10:39.325
None faster than sports,
Kalshi's biggest category.

00:10:40.365 --> 00:10:44.125
During the World Cup, Kalshi
recorded $27 billion in trading

00:10:44.125 --> 00:10:46.505
volume and added three million new users.

00:10:47.345 --> 00:10:48.745
You've talked a lot about

00:10:48.745 --> 00:10:51.686
how Kalshi is diversifying
away from sports,

00:10:51.686 --> 00:10:54.226
but by our count, it's
still 75% of volume.

00:10:54.226 --> 00:10:56.626
So how diverse are you if
sports still pays for this?

00:10:56.626 --> 00:10:57.766
Yeah, I'm not sure if that counts right.

00:10:57.766 --> 00:10:59.346
I mean, depends on how
you count. So it's lower.

00:10:59.346 --> 00:11:02.046
I mean, we are at 95% at
the beginning of the year.

00:11:02.046 --> 00:11:05.120
We're much lower now. I think
it's like two thirds or 70%.

00:11:06.006 --> 00:11:07.926
And so they tend to be an
effective way for people

00:11:07.926 --> 00:11:10.886
to basically come into the
product, experience it,

00:11:10.886 --> 00:11:12.946
understand it, and then they
start exploring other things.

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:16.746
Sports betting is legal
in nearly 40 states,

00:11:16.746 --> 00:11:18.826
but those operators pay millions in state

00:11:18.826 --> 00:11:20.606
licenses and taxes.

00:11:20.606 --> 00:11:24.066
Kalshi doesn't. What are Kalshi's biggest

00:11:24.066 --> 00:11:26.006
threats at this point?

00:11:26.006 --> 00:11:27.106
Well, the states.

00:11:28.306 --> 00:11:32.126
Particularly the gambling
companies based in the states

00:11:32.126 --> 00:11:34.286
and regulated by the states.

00:11:34.286 --> 00:11:37.186
Obviously the gambling
companies want to make money,

00:11:37.186 --> 00:11:39.326
the states want to raise taxes,

00:11:39.326 --> 00:11:41.946
and so they are probably the biggest

00:11:41.946 --> 00:11:44.026
antagonist to Kalshi right now.

00:11:44.026 --> 00:11:45.906
Without sports, what is Kalshi?

00:11:47.746 --> 00:11:52.546
A much smaller company.
Kalshi operates as an exchange

00:11:52.546 --> 00:11:54.506
and says it's different from sports books,

00:11:54.506 --> 00:11:56.546
which take the opposite side of bets

00:11:56.546 --> 00:11:58.646
and win when their betters lose.

00:11:58.646 --> 00:12:01.086
Opponents argue Kalshi
is a gambling platform

00:12:01.086 --> 00:12:03.240
that has found a legal loophole.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.266
44 states have signed a letter
calling prediction markets a

00:12:06.266 --> 00:12:09.546
new form of casino, and 20
are fighting in court over

00:12:09.546 --> 00:12:11.306
whether state sports betting laws apply.

00:12:12.486 --> 00:12:13.946
The CFTC has responded

00:12:13.946 --> 00:12:16.786
by suing states right back
in what's become a full-blown

00:12:16.786 --> 00:12:19.406
turf war over who should
regulate prediction markets.

00:12:20.526 --> 00:12:22.986
What happens to Kalshi if
sports betting goes away?

00:12:22.986 --> 00:12:25.466
It's hard to imagine a
world where you're going

00:12:25.466 --> 00:12:29.166
to carve out specific topics

00:12:29.166 --> 00:12:31.866
because some people believe
that's okay versus not others.

00:12:31.866 --> 00:12:34.206
And then the structure
is sort of the key thing

00:12:34.206 --> 00:12:36.286
that determines how we
should be regulated.

00:12:36.286 --> 00:12:38.007
What we're focused on is making
sure that people understand

00:12:38.007 --> 00:12:40.327
that difference between
an exchange and how it

00:12:40.327 --> 00:12:43.087
works
and a sportsbook and how it works

00:12:43.087 --> 00:12:44.707
and why they're different,
like one is federally regulated

00:12:44.707 --> 00:12:46.547
and one is regulated at the state level.

00:12:46.547 --> 00:12:48.627
Why isn't Kalshi a
casino in your pocket?

00:12:49.567 --> 00:12:50.867
It looks and feels like gambling.

00:12:50.867 --> 00:12:52.767
It looks like someone's speculating,

00:12:52.767 --> 00:12:55.007
feels like it's like a form of gambling,

00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:56.627
but there's a key distinction
and key difference.

00:12:56.627 --> 00:12:58.887
On Kalshi, when customers
lose, they don't lose it to me.

00:12:58.887 --> 00:13:00.107
They lose it to other customers.

00:13:00.107 --> 00:13:02.227
So my role is to establish
fair rules of the game

00:13:03.067 --> 00:13:04.747
for the marketplace to thrive.

00:13:04.747 --> 00:13:06.587
But you do take a fee on every trade.

00:13:06.587 --> 00:13:08.207
Yeah, we're not a nonprofit.

00:13:08.207 --> 00:13:10.387
New York Stock Exchange takes
a fee on every trade, right?

00:13:10.387 --> 00:13:12.547
We take 0.8%, they take something similar.

00:13:12.547 --> 00:13:14.287
That doesn't make New York
Stock Exchange gambling.

00:13:14.287 --> 00:13:15.847
But the more people bet,
the more money you make,

00:13:15.847 --> 00:13:17.027
so your incentive is volume.

00:13:17.027 --> 00:13:18.767
Do you have a financial interest in

00:13:19.607 --> 00:13:22.507
more people betting more, whether
or not it's good for them?

00:13:23.927 --> 00:13:25.767
I have a financial interest
in having more users, yes.

00:13:25.767 --> 00:13:27.567
The same way that the NASDAQ
has a financial interest in

00:13:27.567 --> 00:13:28.887
having more users and more volume.

00:13:28.887 --> 00:13:30.967
I mean, NASDAQ is not figuring
out who are their winners

00:13:30.967 --> 00:13:32.647
and who are their losers
and treat them differently.

00:13:38.027 --> 00:13:39.987
Do you know what a prediction market is?

00:13:39.987 --> 00:13:44.627
It's something that's
come out of gambling

00:13:44.627 --> 00:13:46.367
and trading too.

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:48.927
And then it's just something
that people are using just

00:13:48.927 --> 00:13:50.427
to pass the time.

00:13:51.447 --> 00:13:53.267
Is it gambling or is it trading?

00:13:53.267 --> 00:13:54.587
It's the same thing as gambling.

00:13:54.587 --> 00:13:56.867
I mean, it's just essentially
you could bet on anything.

00:13:56.867 --> 00:13:59.827
Like 20, 30 years ago, the
generation had to go out

00:13:59.827 --> 00:14:01.427
and go out of their own house to gamble

00:14:01.427 --> 00:14:02.547
where now you can just wake up

00:14:02.547 --> 00:14:05.507
and 10 seconds later you're gambling.

00:14:05.507 --> 00:14:07.027
I know it's affecting my generation bad.

00:14:07.027 --> 00:14:09.087
I mean, I know friends
that are addicted to it

00:14:09.087 --> 00:14:10.287
and it ruins lives.

00:14:10.287 --> 00:14:11.507
Does it sound like gambling to you?

00:14:13.447 --> 00:14:17.867
I think that if you
exercise restraint...

00:14:17.867 --> 00:14:18.927
I think it can go either way. I think

00:14:18.927 --> 00:14:20.907
that if you're doing it as like a

00:14:20.907 --> 00:14:22.247
fun hobby, then absolutely.

00:14:22.247 --> 00:14:24.827
So if it's vibes based, it's gambling.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.768
I think for your average
user, the experience

00:14:29.768 --> 00:14:32.808
of using a prediction market
is pretty much identical

00:14:32.808 --> 00:14:35.908
to using a gambling app, which
is why this argument that,

00:14:35.908 --> 00:14:39.068
oh, it's not sports gambling,
it's derivatives contracts,

00:14:39.068 --> 00:14:41.888
event contracts, those
are kind of technicalities

00:14:41.888 --> 00:14:45.088
that might pass muster in a court,

00:14:45.088 --> 00:14:48.088
but I think for your average
person just don't really

00:14:48.088 --> 00:14:49.628
pass the smell test.

00:14:49.628 --> 00:14:52.828
The question of whether
Kalshi is a financial exchange

00:14:52.828 --> 00:14:54.808
or a gambling company may ultimately be

00:14:54.808 --> 00:14:56.548
decided by the highest court.

00:14:56.548 --> 00:14:58.268
Does the Supreme Court take up the case of

00:14:58.268 --> 00:15:00.648
who should regulate prediction markets?

00:15:00.648 --> 00:15:01.648
Yes or no?

00:15:03.508 --> 00:15:05.148
I'd probably say yes.
I don't know.

00:15:05.148 --> 00:15:07.168
That's a question for
lawyers. I mean, I don't know.

00:15:08.188 --> 00:15:10.128
Trump has made it clear
he thinks this is the

00:15:10.128 --> 00:15:11.228
federal government's turf.

00:15:12.148 --> 00:15:13.788
His son, Donald Trump Jr.

00:15:13.788 --> 00:15:17.248
is an investor in Polymarket
and a paid advisor to Kalshi.

00:15:17.248 --> 00:15:21.508
In 2025, he received a
$300,000 equity stake in Kalshi

00:15:21.508 --> 00:15:23.428
that's now potentially worth millions.

00:15:24.288 --> 00:15:27.708
I don't think we've ever had the family

00:15:27.708 --> 00:15:28.908
of a sitting president being

00:15:28.908 --> 00:15:32.548
so closely involved in a financial
instrument that's growing

00:15:32.548 --> 00:15:33.768
so fast,

00:15:33.768 --> 00:15:38.128
and that clearly has a lot of
regulatory questions raised.

00:15:38.128 --> 00:15:42.008
The CFTC has been incredibly
defensive, so I think

00:15:42.008 --> 00:15:44.288
that's the dynamic
that, to certain extent,

00:15:44.288 --> 00:15:46.328
is facing some scrutiny.

00:15:46.328 --> 00:15:49.368
How often do you personally
talk to Donald Trump Jr.?

00:15:49.368 --> 00:15:52.308
Not very often. One specific
thing he's advised you on?

00:15:52.308 --> 00:15:53.928
It was really always
focused about going direct

00:15:53.928 --> 00:15:56.968
and talking to our customers
and people directly.

00:15:56.968 --> 00:15:59.128
We don't talk about regulatory or policy.

00:15:59.128 --> 00:16:01.308
It was always made clear from
the start by both parties

00:16:01.308 --> 00:16:03.508
that there will not be any
involvement on that front.

00:16:04.548 --> 00:16:06.468
Earlier this year, Donald Trump Jr.

00:16:06.468 --> 00:16:08.448
reportedly showed up at
a closed door gathering

00:16:08.448 --> 00:16:10.268
of Republican Attorneys General

00:16:10.268 --> 00:16:12.729
and told them to back
off prediction markets.

00:16:12.729 --> 00:16:14.448
The controversy hasn't slowed down

00:16:14.448 --> 00:16:16.068
Kalshi's marketing machine.

00:16:16.068 --> 00:16:18.249
Chasing new users,
they've aggressively gone

00:16:18.249 --> 00:16:21.389
after Gen Z with celebrity
ads, viral TikToks,

00:16:21.389 --> 00:16:22.389
and influencer deals.

00:16:26.249 --> 00:16:28.209
You will be the first
millionaire in your family.

00:16:28.209 --> 00:16:29.749
Oh my God, they're going over.

00:16:29.749 --> 00:16:33.169
No. Prediction market traders
often focus on a category

00:16:33.169 --> 00:16:34.789
where they think they have an edge.

00:16:34.789 --> 00:16:36.909
What are you trading on Kalshi now mostly?

00:16:36.909 --> 00:16:39.749
Just the weather. I usually
just do high temperatures.

00:16:39.749 --> 00:16:42.829
I had a theory last year where
while it was cooling down,

00:16:42.829 --> 00:16:44.829
people were underestimating
the temperature would be

00:16:44.829 --> 00:16:46.389
consistently, and

00:16:46.389 --> 00:16:48.409
for a few days in a row
it was actually easy

00:16:48.409 --> 00:16:49.829
to cash in on that.

00:16:49.829 --> 00:16:51.209
Yet traders like Joseph,

00:16:51.209 --> 00:16:53.969
who win more often than
they lose, are the minority.

00:16:53.969 --> 00:16:57.189
Do you worry about young
people getting hooked?

00:16:57.189 --> 00:16:58.529
Irresponsible users are a real worry

00:16:58.529 --> 00:16:59.849
for any of these types of products.

00:16:59.849 --> 00:17:03.029
The law today is 18 plus,
and I follow the law.

00:17:03.029 --> 00:17:04.609
And so that question is also

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:05.969
better addressed to policymakers.

00:17:05.969 --> 00:17:09.389
Like if policymakers are
supportive to move that to 21 plus,

00:17:09.389 --> 00:17:10.869
that is something that we would respect.

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:14.409
There are influencers all
over social media saying they

00:17:14.409 --> 00:17:15.689
made easy money on Kalshi

00:17:15.689 --> 00:17:19.429
but when you look at the numbers,
75% of people lose money.

00:17:19.429 --> 00:17:22.229
Do you feel an obligation,
a moral obligation

00:17:22.229 --> 00:17:23.629
to be more transparent about that?

00:17:24.489 --> 00:17:27.429
I think we are very transparent
that there are risks in

00:17:28.449 --> 00:17:30.129
every trading product.

00:17:30.129 --> 00:17:32.769
There are risks. I would
say though that the 75%,

00:17:32.769 --> 00:17:34.449
what's interesting to me is

00:17:34.449 --> 00:17:36.389
that actually it's never quoted next to

00:17:37.509 --> 00:17:39.249
how many people lose money day trading.

00:17:39.249 --> 00:17:40.329
I think these day trading options get

00:17:40.329 --> 00:17:42.509
95% of retail loses money.

00:17:42.509 --> 00:17:43.689
What do you make of their argument?

00:17:44.949 --> 00:17:47.669
Well, yeah, it's common knowledge

00:17:47.669 --> 00:17:49.969
that day traders lose a lot of money, and

00:17:49.969 --> 00:17:52.829
therefore I was taught
from an early age not

00:17:52.829 --> 00:17:54.269
to try to day trade.

00:17:55.529 --> 00:17:58.369
We're seeing some recent
studies say people are more

00:17:58.369 --> 00:18:01.010
likely to allocate some of
the money they'll actually put

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:03.029
to investing and put it in sports betting.

00:18:03.029 --> 00:18:06.030
So I think the most important
thing is the money you put

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.870
there, where is it coming from?

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:11.110
Is it coming from money that's
allocated for your future,

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:12.490
money that's for investments, money

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.330
that potentially in the long term,

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:15.550
if you put in different vehicles,

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:17.930
can give you better returns?

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.090
This year alone, users between 18

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:21.850
and 21 who would be too young

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:24.490
to walk into a casino
in most states traded $5

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.210
billion on Kalshi.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.090
We are a financial exchange

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.470
and we follow the same rules
that everyone else does,

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:31.710
but I think if those rules are

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:34.210
to be changed, we'll follow the new rules.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:37.630
But for us, what we think
is better is never trying to

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:38.690
stop something from happening

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:41.330
because the people would just go offshore

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:44.910
or do it in another way that
is unregulated and way worse.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:48.090
At what point do you stop
pointing to a worse option

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:49.810
and take responsibility?

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:51.090
We're not just here saying like, "Okay,

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:52.310
there's a worse option so we don't have

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:53.850
to do anything." I'm actually here saying,

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.210
"Let's talk about regulation.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.790
Let's figure out what
are the right regulations

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:00.050
and figure out the right
safeguards." But we are actually

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:01.210
welcoming that conversation.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.710
And when we talk to policymakers,
our message is not, "Hey,

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:04.630
there's nothing to see here." Our

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:05.650
message is like, "Let's regulate it.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:06.530
Let's figure out what are the right

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:08.090
guardrails and where are the gaps?"

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:11.890
There's just this huge
settlement with Meta,

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:14.790
massive numbers, restrictions
on their platforms now.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.270
Do you see any parallels there?

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.810
There are parallels that
any type of technology

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:21.110
comes with risks, for sure.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:22.770
Like AI comes with risks.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:24.910
In our market, and this
is true for any type

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:26.450
of financial markets, it's
true for options, it's true

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:27.710
for futures, it's true for stock

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:28.830
market, it's true for prediction markets.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:30.890
There will be a small percentage

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:32.450
of customers when you enable them

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:34.230
to speculate on different things

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:36.090
that will be irresponsible users,

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:37.210
like irresponsible trading.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.790
But critics say platforms
like Kalshi should create more

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:43.450
safeguards for their youngest
users, some who've learned

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:45.790
to gamble before they've learned to save.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.310
It'll start off, you know,

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:49.730
you'll make a few bucks here and there.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:52.531
You'll think it's easy money.
It just goes down from there.

00:19:52.531 --> 00:19:55.011
How much have you lost?
Like 5K.

00:19:56.131 --> 00:19:58.491
Okay.
Things are so expensive nowadays.

00:19:58.491 --> 00:19:59.851
The only chance they have is gambling

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:01.851
and that's just not a good mindset

00:20:01.851 --> 00:20:03.631
and they made it so like accessible.

00:20:05.451 --> 00:20:07.431
Even as concerns grow
about prediction markets

00:20:07.431 --> 00:20:10.051
and gambling addiction,
Kalshi is doubling down on the

00:20:10.051 --> 00:20:13.271
category that arguably
started it all, elections.

00:20:14.511 --> 00:20:15.831
Elections really are the holy

00:20:15.831 --> 00:20:16.951
grail of prediction markets, right?

00:20:16.951 --> 00:20:18.591
They are the one that proves

00:20:18.591 --> 00:20:19.991
to everyone what these markets are.

00:20:19.991 --> 00:20:21.671
They prove the utility of these markets,

00:20:21.671 --> 00:20:23.831
and they are also the one
that provide the most value.

00:20:25.091 --> 00:20:28.371
With the 2026 midterms, Kalshi
is making a play to be seen

00:20:28.371 --> 00:20:30.951
as a go-to source for election odds.

00:20:30.951 --> 00:20:33.251
Critics say it's more
likely to add confusion

00:20:33.251 --> 00:20:36.131
and give bad actors a new
tool to spread misinformation.

00:20:37.051 --> 00:20:38.851
Okay, so this is the midterms hub?

00:20:38.851 --> 00:20:40.351
Oh yeah. So what we did is we

00:20:40.351 --> 00:20:41.991
broke this down into three...

00:20:41.991 --> 00:20:44.451
Couple themes here. Like you'll
see Senate, house, governor,

00:20:44.451 --> 00:20:45.731
balance of power, which is an

00:20:45.731 --> 00:20:47.931
amalgamation of all these combined.

00:20:47.931 --> 00:20:50.991
We love showing like how
things change over time

00:20:50.991 --> 00:20:52.311
as more data comes through.

00:20:53.931 --> 00:20:56.051
Kalshi's midterms
dashboard certainly looks

00:20:56.051 --> 00:20:58.011
official, but look closely.

00:20:58.011 --> 00:21:00.911
Those numbers aren't
polling data, they're bets.

00:21:00.911 --> 00:21:02.391
And with elections, the stakes

00:21:02.391 --> 00:21:04.891
of getting manipulated
aren't just financial.

00:21:04.891 --> 00:21:07.351
They go to the integrity
of the vote itself.

00:21:07.351 --> 00:21:08.931
Polls do have a credibility problem,

00:21:08.931 --> 00:21:11.351
but our prediction market's
really that much better.

00:21:11.351 --> 00:21:13.251
When you put money on the table,

00:21:13.251 --> 00:21:14.451
things become more complicated.

00:21:14.451 --> 00:21:15.531
What if I have an incentive

00:21:15.531 --> 00:21:17.871
to push a certain candidate's odds

00:21:17.871 --> 00:21:19.351
higher than there certainly are?

00:21:19.351 --> 00:21:22.351
What we see is that in smaller
races, state representative

00:21:22.351 --> 00:21:25.851
or mayoral elections,
it can take as little

00:21:25.851 --> 00:21:28.771
as a few thousand or even
a few hundred dollars to

00:21:28.771 --> 00:21:31.471
push a candidate's odds up or down.

00:21:31.471 --> 00:21:35.691
In January, an anonymous
polymarket trader spent $44,000

00:21:35.691 --> 00:21:40.051
to push a California gubernatorial
candidate's odds to 96%,

00:21:40.051 --> 00:21:42.200
even though polls had him at less than 5%.

00:21:43.752 --> 00:21:48.132
A recent study found that
for $3,500, you can move 97%

00:21:48.132 --> 00:21:51.832
of midterm related prediction
markets by five cents or more.

00:21:51.832 --> 00:21:54.812
That's a remarkably cheap way
to shape public perception,

00:21:54.812 --> 00:21:57.412
especially if those odds are
being displayed on national

00:21:57.412 --> 00:22:00.792
television, visibility that
Kalshi is currently paying for

00:22:00.792 --> 00:22:03.232
through partnerships with
several media organizations.

00:22:04.192 --> 00:22:08.552
Putting prediction market data
next to polls on television,

00:22:08.552 --> 00:22:11.012
do you think this could confuse viewers?

00:22:12.072 --> 00:22:16.512
I think it definitely
creates the question of like,

00:22:16.512 --> 00:22:18.472
is it clearly defined?

00:22:18.472 --> 00:22:19.732
What are the polls saying?

00:22:19.732 --> 00:22:21.452
What are prediction markets saying?

00:22:21.452 --> 00:22:24.252
But having them right next to each other

00:22:24.252 --> 00:22:25.412
and comparing them,

00:22:25.412 --> 00:22:29.172
I think it definitely can
create a lot of issues.

00:22:29.172 --> 00:22:30.652
Do you feel like the stakes
are high with this election?

00:22:30.652 --> 00:22:32.472
I think it's always, you know,

00:22:32.472 --> 00:22:36.372
around any big tent pole
moment, you're always going

00:22:36.372 --> 00:22:38.772
to have more energy and attention

00:22:38.772 --> 00:22:40.932
and all that, which is
good. That's what we want.

00:22:40.932 --> 00:22:42.932
The way I would phrase it
is like the stakes are high

00:22:42.932 --> 00:22:43.892
and the onus anonymous to

00:22:43.892 --> 00:22:45.212
educate people about how to use them.

00:22:46.412 --> 00:22:48.592
Whoever wins or loses in the midterms

00:22:48.592 --> 00:22:51.312
or what the results do to
public confidence in the vote,

00:22:51.312 --> 00:22:52.732
Kalshi will take its cut.

00:22:53.872 --> 00:22:55.532
Ironically, the fickle world

00:22:55.532 --> 00:22:58.392
of political fortunes could
be an existential threat.

00:22:59.232 --> 00:23:01.952
What happens to Kalshi when
Trump leaves office when

00:23:01.952 --> 00:23:03.132
there's a new administration

00:23:04.032 --> 00:23:07.472
and perhaps the regulatory
pendulum swings back?

00:23:07.472 --> 00:23:09.312
You know, we have supporters
and detractors from both

00:23:09.312 --> 00:23:12.392
sides of the aisle,
some people that see it

00:23:12.392 --> 00:23:14.912
and some people that don't
see it or don't see it yet.

00:23:14.912 --> 00:23:17.832
To me, it's really the
focus is like, okay, we have

00:23:17.832 --> 00:23:18.972
tens of millions of customers now.

00:23:18.972 --> 00:23:21.272
This is a fast growing
industry outside of AI.

00:23:21.272 --> 00:23:22.832
It's an industry that's here to stay.

00:23:22.832 --> 00:23:23.753
It's not about whether prediction

00:23:23.753 --> 00:23:25.472
markets going to be here or not.

00:23:25.472 --> 00:23:26.392
We're way past that point.

